<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/867">867</a>–<a href="/years/1056">1056</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Macedonian dynasty ruled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> from 867 to 1056, beginning with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a>. Its era saw military recovery, territorial expansion and a cultural and legal revival. The Byzantine economy and state finances were strong in this period, which is regarded as a high point of the empire.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Macedonian dynasty" width="320" height="397" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: from the Middle Ages, unknown · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Basilios_II.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The dynasty is named after its founder, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> the Macedonian, who came from the theme of Macedonia in the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thrace">Thrace</a>, and it followed the Amorian dynasty. Its ethnic origin is unknown and has been debated. During Basil's reign an elaborate genealogy was produced that claimed his ancestors were not mere peasants, as everyone believed, but descendants of the Arsacid kings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> and of Constantine the Great.</p><p>Some Persian writers, such as Hamza al-Isfahani and al-Tabari, called Basil a Saqlabi, a term usually denoting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> but which can mean the inhabitants of the region between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>. Claims have therefore been made for Armenian, Slavonic or mixed descent. The author of the only dedicated English biography of Basil concludes that his origins cannot be established with certainty, though he relied on the support of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> in prominent positions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-i">Basil I</a> ruled from 867 to 886 and married Eudokia Ingerina, mistress of Michael III; he died in a hunting accident. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leo-vi-the-wise">Leo VI the Wise</a>, possibly the natural son of Michael III, caused a church crisis by his fourth marriage, to Zoe Karbonopsina, who ruled as regent for their son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-vii">Constantine VII</a> from 914 to 919. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-i-lekapenos">Romanos I Lekapenos</a> staged a coup in 919 and became senior emperor in 920, until he was deposed and exiled in 944. Constantine VII, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/born-in-the-purple">born in the purple</a>, ruled again from 945 to 959.</p><p>After Romanos II (959–963) came two successful generals: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nikephoros-ii-phokas">Nikephoros II Phokas</a> (963–969), who was assassinated, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-i-tzimiskes">John I Tzimiskes</a> (969–976), the regents for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>. Basil II, the Bulgar-slayer, reigned from 976 to 1025. His brother Constantine VIII followed as sole emperor until 1028, and then Constantine's daughter Zoe ruled until 1050 with a succession of husbands: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanos-iii-argyros">Romanos III Argyros</a>, who was murdered, Michael IV the Paphlagonian and Constantine IX Monomachos.</p><p>Michael V the Caulker, Zoe's adopted son, reigned in 1041–1042. Zoe's sister Theodora ruled jointly with her in 1042 and alone from 1055 to 1056. Michael VI, chosen by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodora-wife-of-theophilos">Theodora</a>, was a non-dynastic ruler who was deposed and entered a monastery.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Under the dynasty the Byzantine state reached its largest extent since the early Muslim conquests, and the Macedonian Renaissance in letters and arts began. The dynasty's family ties reached beyond the empire: Basil's daughter Anna was betrothed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-the-blind">Louis the Blind</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a>'s sister Anna Porphyrogeneta married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-the-great">Vladimir I of Kiev</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macedonian dynasty” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The recovery under the <b>Macedonian dynasty</b> (867–1056) rested on a smaller but more coherent base: the Anatolian plateau supplied soldiers and grain, the revival of Balkan and Black Sea trade generated customs revenue, and the theme system of military landholding sustained frontier defence.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Phokas took part in three revolts for and against the ruling <b>Macedonian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-13">13 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>She was the last sovereign of the <b>Macedonian dynasty</b>, which had ruled the Byzantine Empire since 867.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-31">31 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>She was the last sovereign of the <b>Macedonian dynasty</b>, which had ruled the empire for almost 200 years.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-11">11 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Fearing a loss of influence, Basil had Michael assassinated the next year, becoming sole ruler and founding the <b>Macedonian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He founded the <b>Macedonian dynasty</b>, which ruled for nearly two centuries.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/september-24">24 September in history</a></li></ul></section>
